# =============================================================================
# ↦ — An unhandled length mismatch ends the run.
# =============================================================================
#
# What this teaches:
#   • The `main` body is exempt from the handler rule, so a bare `ys ↦ tensor<int, [4]>` compiles; when `ys` does not hold exactly four items the conversion fails with nothing to catch it.
#   • The run prints the output written before the failure, then one trap line on stderr, and exits with the conversion class (65, EX_DATAERR) from the trap contract.
#
# Common mistakes:
#   • Treating `main` as a handler — the same conversion inside `fac … cape`, behind `⊥`, or in a `⇥ string` function is catchable (see lista-tensor-length-fallible.fab).
#
# See also: ↦, ⇥, ⊥, fac, cape, tensor, Figura
# =============================================================================

# EXPECTED failure:
#   before
#   (stderr) error: tensor conversion expected 4 elements, got 1

main {
    let list<int> short ← [1]
    print "before"
    let tensor<int, [4]> t ← short ↦ tensor<int, [4]>
    print t.sum()
}
